{
  "result": "Morning arrives quietly in the small coastal town, slipping through curtains like a thief who has decided to return what was never stolen. The baker opens his shop before dawn, and the scent of warm bread drifts down narrow streets where cats still sleep against stone walls. I walk without hurry, because hurry belongs to other lives, other calendars, other people who measure worth in minutes.\n\nThe sea is not blue at this hour but silver, gray, and somehow hopeful. Fishing boats rock gently against the harbor, their lanterns swinging like small moons refusing to set. An old woman feeds gulls from a bench, tossing crumbs with quiet generosity. She nods at me, as if we have known each other for years.\n\nI think about how places teach us to breathe. Here, the wind sets the tempo. You cannot rush a tide. You cannot argue with fog. You learn to wait for light, to mend nets, to listen to stories that grow taller each winter. Everything important happens slowly, and then all at once: a catch, a storm, a child learning to swim, a letter arriving after twenty years.\n\nBy noon the town will be ordinary again, with tourists and laughter and salt on every windowsill. But for now, the world feels unfinished, and that is a kind of mercy. I carry the quiet with me into the day, not as a burden, but as a promise that I was here, awake, when the light first decided to finally stay.",
